1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,630
You click a button. Nothing happens.
You click it again. Still nothing.

2
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:07,360
You move the mouse. Nothing.

3
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:10,740
For a few seconds, your
computer looks completely dead.

4
00:00:11,519 --> 00:00:16,209
And then everything happens at once.
The window moves. The menu opens.

5
00:00:16,339 --> 00:00:18,824
Every click you made
arrives, all together.

6
00:00:20,673 --> 00:00:22,323
Here is the strange part.

7
00:00:23,073 --> 00:00:24,583
Your computer knew you clicked.

8
00:00:25,173 --> 00:00:26,683
It knew within microseconds.

9
00:00:27,579 --> 00:00:32,659
That is not a guess. Right now, with you
doing nothing in particular, your machine

10
00:00:32,739 --> 00:00:35,969
is dealing with thousands of
hardware events every second.

11
00:00:36,786 --> 00:00:40,826
A key going down. A packet
arriving. A timer firing.

12
00:00:40,906 --> 00:00:42,396
A disk finishing a job.

13
00:00:43,156 --> 00:00:46,036
Each one of those is an
interrupt, and computers have

14
00:00:46,116 --> 00:00:50,186
worked this way since the
1950s, for exactly this purpose:

15
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:54,247
so that a processor could react
to the world outside itself.

16
00:00:55,103 --> 00:00:57,113
So the hardware reported your click.

17
00:00:57,863 --> 00:00:59,313
The processor noticed.

18
00:01:00,123 --> 00:01:03,373
And you still sat there for five
seconds staring at a dead screen.

19
00:01:04,307 --> 00:01:05,937
Shouldn't the user come first?

20
00:01:06,883 --> 00:01:10,693
There is an answer, and it is not
that your computer is too slow.

21
00:01:11,437 --> 00:01:15,367
It is that being noticed and being served
are two completely different things.

22
00:01:18,227 --> 00:01:19,957
Let's start with the noticing.

23
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:22,837
Imagine a computer without interrupts.

24
00:01:23,765 --> 00:01:28,395
The processor would have to ask, over and
over: Did the keyboard receive anything?

25
00:01:28,765 --> 00:01:29,965
Did the disk finish?

26
00:01:30,669 --> 00:01:32,449
Did a packet arrive? Did the mouse move?

27
00:01:32,529 --> 00:01:34,559
And then ask again. And again.

28
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:40,180
Almost every answer would be no, and the
asking itself would eat the machine alive.

29
00:01:41,037 --> 00:01:43,427
So devices got a way to raise a hand.

30
00:01:44,317 --> 00:01:47,469
The mouse moves, the
hardware raises an interrupt,

31
00:01:47,919 --> 00:01:50,369
and the processor drops
whatever it was doing,

32
00:01:50,539 --> 00:01:53,309
jumps into a small piece
of code called a handler,

33
00:01:54,116 --> 00:01:57,346
and the operating system finds
out that something happened.

34
00:01:58,159 --> 00:01:59,919
It is a brilliant idea.

35
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:02,949
And it leads to a very
reasonable assumption.

36
00:02:03,910 --> 00:02:07,320
If the processor can be pulled
away from anything, instantly,

37
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:10,460
then surely my click gets
dealt with instantly too.

38
00:02:11,217 --> 00:02:14,147
Except the handler is not
allowed to do anything much.

39
00:02:15,003 --> 00:02:18,313
While the processor is inside
it, everything else is waiting.

40
00:02:19,443 --> 00:02:23,213
Including the next interrupt. And
the next one is already coming.

41
00:02:23,978 --> 00:02:26,548
So the rule is the opposite
of what you would expect.

42
00:02:27,458 --> 00:02:29,988
The handler does as
little as it possibly can.

43
00:02:30,753 --> 00:02:35,083
It notes what happened. It tells
the device that the message arrived.

44
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:37,373
It drops the event into a queue.

45
00:02:37,453 --> 00:02:38,903
And then it gets out.

46
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:43,687
Kernel engineers even split the
work into two named halves: the

47
00:02:43,767 --> 00:02:47,077
part that must happen now, and
the part that can happen later.

48
00:02:47,909 --> 00:02:52,199
Everything interesting, everything you
would ever see, is in the second half.

49
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:54,945
Which gives us the first answer.

50
00:02:55,675 --> 00:02:57,785
An interrupt gets the
computer's attention.

51
00:02:58,855 --> 00:03:01,325
It does not get your
program onto a processor.

52
00:03:04,565 --> 00:03:07,435
Fine, you might say. Then fix that.

53
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:10,795
Make input the most important
thing in the building.

54
00:03:11,599 --> 00:03:14,459
Mouse? Highest priority. Keyboard?

55
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:15,759
Highest priority.

56
00:03:15,839 --> 00:03:17,549
The window in front of you?

57
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:19,229
Highest priority.

58
00:03:19,999 --> 00:03:21,909
Everything else can wait its turn.

59
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:24,129
It sounds obviously right.

60
00:03:26,409 --> 00:03:29,259
And the best argument
against it happened on Mars.

61
00:03:30,153 --> 00:03:34,983
In 1997, the Pathfinder lander
touched down and started working.

62
00:03:35,453 --> 00:03:38,523
Then, days later, it
began resetting itself.

63
00:03:39,425 --> 00:03:43,015
Not a cosmic ray. Not a broken
part. A scheduling problem.

64
00:03:43,809 --> 00:03:46,689
There was a high priority
task that moved data around

65
00:03:46,769 --> 00:03:50,179
the spacecraft, and it needed
a shared piece of memory.

66
00:03:50,945 --> 00:03:53,385
A low priority task
collecting weather data

67
00:03:53,465 --> 00:03:56,115
had got there first and was holding it.

68
00:03:56,758 --> 00:03:58,628
Which would have been fine for a moment.

69
00:03:59,398 --> 00:04:02,348
But a medium priority task
then took the processor,

70
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:06,351
and the low priority one
never got a chance to finish.

71
00:04:06,681 --> 00:04:08,431
So it never let go.

72
00:04:09,242 --> 00:04:11,222
The most important task on the spacecraft

73
00:04:11,302 --> 00:04:14,692
was now stuck behind
the least important one.

74
00:04:15,333 --> 00:04:17,503
It has a name. Priority inversion.

75
00:04:20,043 --> 00:04:22,503
A watchdog timer noticed
that the important task

76
00:04:22,583 --> 00:04:26,153
had not run for far too
long, assumed disaster,

77
00:04:27,051 --> 00:04:31,521
and reset the entire spacecraft.
Then it happened again. And again.

78
00:04:32,354 --> 00:04:36,344
Engineers on Earth worked out
why, and sent a fix up to Mars.

79
00:04:37,229 --> 00:04:39,759
And the lesson costs
nothing to carry home.

80
00:04:40,509 --> 00:04:43,059
High priority does not
mean you get served.

81
00:04:43,833 --> 00:04:45,883
It only means you get asked first.

82
00:04:46,793 --> 00:04:51,163
If the thing you need is held by somebody
slower, your importance buys you nothing.

83
00:04:53,873 --> 00:04:55,943
And priority has a second limit.

84
00:04:56,633 --> 00:04:58,323
It decides who goes first.

85
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:01,643
It does not create a single extra cycle.

86
00:05:02,421 --> 00:05:05,541
Suppose every core on your
machine is at one hundred percent.

87
00:05:05,621 --> 00:05:08,111
You might think that means frozen.

88
00:05:09,041 --> 00:05:10,431
It usually doesn't.

89
00:05:11,097 --> 00:05:15,967
The scheduler hands out slices of time so
quickly that you cannot feel the joins.

90
00:05:16,911 --> 00:05:19,361
That is why you can
compile something enormous

91
00:05:19,571 --> 00:05:21,781
and still scroll a web page while it runs.

92
00:05:22,639 --> 00:05:25,249
What changes under load
is not really speed.

93
00:05:26,039 --> 00:05:27,589
The computer is not slow.

94
00:05:28,433 --> 00:05:29,633
It is late.

95
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:37,603
You click, and the answer comes half a
second later. Then a second. Then five.

96
00:05:38,518 --> 00:05:41,528
And people are extraordinarily
good at noticing that.

97
00:05:42,298 --> 00:05:44,948
Half a second feels like a
machine that is thinking.

98
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,090
Five feels like a machine that has died.

99
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:53,730
And there is a version of this
that surprises people most.

100
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:57,330
Sometimes the processor
is not the problem at all.

101
00:05:58,043 --> 00:06:01,833
Open too many things at once
and physical memory fills up.

102
00:06:02,643 --> 00:06:06,493
The operating system starts shuffling
pages of memory out to storage.

103
00:06:07,315 --> 00:06:09,615
Do enough of that and the
machine spends its time

104
00:06:09,695 --> 00:06:12,805
moving memory around
instead of doing your work.

105
00:06:13,533 --> 00:06:15,443
It has a name too. Thrashing.

106
00:06:16,296 --> 00:06:20,846
And this is the confusing one, because
the processor might not look busy at all.

107
00:06:21,576 --> 00:06:23,746
It is not working. It is waiting.

108
00:06:24,701 --> 00:06:27,381
You can hand a waiting task
the highest priority on

109
00:06:27,461 --> 00:06:30,771
the system and it will wait
at exactly the same speed.

110
00:06:33,401 --> 00:06:37,711
And then there is the most human
version of this. The event arrived.

111
00:06:38,441 --> 00:06:39,641
The kernel has it.

112
00:06:40,328 --> 00:06:43,258
It is sitting in a queue with
your program's name on it.

113
00:06:43,788 --> 00:06:45,978
And your program is not picking it up.

114
00:06:46,719 --> 00:06:49,339
Maybe it is doing a long
calculation on the same

115
00:06:49,419 --> 00:06:52,809
thread that is supposed to
be watching its own window.

116
00:06:53,621 --> 00:06:55,971
So the mouse works. The keyboard works.

117
00:06:56,401 --> 00:06:57,751
Other programs work.

118
00:06:58,519 --> 00:07:02,129
And that one window turns pale
and says it is not responding.

119
00:07:02,924 --> 00:07:04,594
Which is not a diagnosis.

120
00:07:05,684 --> 00:07:08,064
Windows puts that label
on a window after about

121
00:07:08,144 --> 00:07:10,934
five seconds of nobody
collecting its messages.

122
00:07:11,897 --> 00:07:13,947
The system is perfectly alive.

123
00:07:14,677 --> 00:07:17,207
The application simply
has not come to the door.

124
00:07:17,989 --> 00:07:20,209
The computer receiving your click and the

125
00:07:20,289 --> 00:07:23,059
application answering it
are not the same event.

126
00:07:23,952 --> 00:07:25,572
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

127
00:07:25,652 --> 00:07:28,522
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

128
00:07:29,432 --> 00:07:30,842
It really helps the channel.

129
00:07:31,645 --> 00:07:35,155
There is one more link, and it
is the one we never think about.

130
00:07:35,725 --> 00:07:39,215
Nothing so far has actually
put anything on your screen.

131
00:07:39,935 --> 00:07:42,085
The application changes its state.

132
00:07:42,835 --> 00:07:44,825
Something has to build the next frame.

133
00:07:45,655 --> 00:07:47,685
The graphics card has to draw it.

134
00:07:48,175 --> 00:07:49,925
The display has to show it.

135
00:07:50,618 --> 00:07:55,028
On an ordinary screen a new picture
appears about every sixteen milliseconds.

136
00:07:56,154 --> 00:07:57,734
So there is a difference
between the computer

137
00:07:57,814 --> 00:08:00,644
dealing with your click
and you seeing that it did.

138
00:08:03,224 --> 00:08:07,354
And underneath all of it sits the part
that no amount of engineering fixes.

139
00:08:08,544 --> 00:08:11,274
The operating system knows
that your mouse moved.

140
00:08:12,024 --> 00:08:13,494
It has no idea why.

141
00:08:14,268 --> 00:08:16,318
You might be aiming at
something in a game.

142
00:08:16,928 --> 00:08:18,278
Or editing a document.

143
00:08:18,828 --> 00:08:20,598
Or you might have knocked the desk.

144
00:08:21,387 --> 00:08:23,557
To the hardware, those are the same event.

145
00:08:24,487 --> 00:08:26,437
A system can rank kinds of work.

146
00:08:26,727 --> 00:08:28,617
It cannot guess what you meant.

147
00:08:29,444 --> 00:08:30,754
So it compromises.

148
00:08:31,644 --> 00:08:35,234
Fast enough for you, most of the time,
without starving everything else.

149
00:08:37,964 --> 00:08:40,134
Which brings us back to that dead screen.

150
00:08:40,864 --> 00:08:42,554
Your click was never one event.

151
00:08:43,459 --> 00:08:45,349
The hardware has to report it.

152
00:08:45,819 --> 00:08:47,529
The kernel has to store it.

153
00:08:48,079 --> 00:08:49,809
The queue has to reach your program.

154
00:08:50,685 --> 00:08:52,235
Your program has to run.

155
00:08:52,945 --> 00:08:54,575
The interface has to change.

156
00:08:55,385 --> 00:08:59,175
Something has to draw the new picture,
and the screen has to show it to you.

157
00:08:59,830 --> 00:09:02,400
Every one of those
steps is small and fast.

158
00:09:03,297 --> 00:09:07,287
Delay any single one of them and
you don't experience a slow window.

159
00:09:08,107 --> 00:09:09,997
You experience a dead computer.

160
00:09:12,947 --> 00:09:16,737
And the strangest thing about the whole
business is how rarely it happens.

161
00:09:17,609 --> 00:09:20,379
We expect a machine running
billions of operations

162
00:09:20,849 --> 00:09:23,239
a second to answer a twitch of the hand.

163
00:09:24,041 --> 00:09:25,831
And almost always, it does.

164
00:09:27,318 --> 00:09:30,728
So the next time you click and
nothing happens, give it a moment.

165
00:09:31,531 --> 00:09:32,731
It heard you.

166
00:09:33,911 --> 00:09:36,061
It just had a queue, and you were in it.
